            <ArticleTitle>Aspirin: action on receptor in the tooth.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>180-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Impulse frequency recorded from dentinal receptors in the cat's tooth provided an indication of sensory excitation. Topical application of aspirin to the dentin promptly inhibited both steady-state discharge and response to a brief heat stimulus. When the drug was washed out, the initial excitability was restored in 15 minutes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
